MULTIPLE DOCKER CONTAINERS:
• Use multiple Docker containers to isolate tasks and maintain consistent environments.
• Containers solve dependency conflicts and save setup time by packaging apps with their dependencies.
• Containers are lightweight, fast, and allow running multiple isolated instances on one host.
• Docker Compose orchestrates multiple containers for microservices that communicate independently.
• Multiple Compose files enable environment-specific setups for staging, development, and production.

DEFINE MULTIPLE DOCKER CONTAINERS:
• Use a docker-compose.yml file to define and manage multiple Docker containers in one solution.
• Containers run in isolation by default; configure networking to enable inter-container communication.
• Each container should focus on a single responsibility for easier versioning, scalability, and maintenance.
• Docker Compose reads docker-compose.yml and optional docker-compose.override.yml files to configure multi-container applications.
• Deploy all containers together with one command using docker-compose up for consistent environments and simplified management.

